- Recovery economics unchanged: every hang = reboot + ladder (~3 min).

Identical-TD chain test (final, same evening): two IDENTICAL gemm TDs
sharing ONE 0x80000 kernel blob (concat-style kernel table, no per-TD
offsets), proven envelope: TIMEOUT. Chaining is blocked at the TD-family
level, not per-TD kernel addressing: concat-family (elementwise pass-
through) TDs chain; conv/gemm-family TDs do not, under the same envelope.
Suspects narrowed to TD-family config (W6 engine flags) or the stab's
declared bank regions, both requiring macOS IOKit RE or stab semantics
study. Device recovered (reboot + ladder, ANE_VERIFIED).

Task-header decode (same session, final addendum). The payload's first
0x28 bytes are a TASK HEADER mirroring TD_0's W0..W9 register words (proof:
golden model.hwx header words = 0x02000000/0/0x422/0/0xFFF86A/0/0x30009800
= exactly its TD's W2/W4/W6 values; the 0xF401F800 "magic" at +0x28 is just
the KernelDMA stream header (61<<26)|0x1F800, not a magic constant).
Empirics on the 2-TD gemm chain:
- header = concat copy (conv-family values): TIMEOUT.
- header mirrored to gemm W-values: task COMPLETES cleanly (no timeout) but
  writes no output - the TM now accepts and walks the chain.
- hybrid (concat header + concat banks + gemm TD bodies): completes, no
  writes.
Interpretation: the header programs the task's engine/DMAC context; a
mismatched header wedges the fetch, a matching one runs the chain through
completion. The remaining gap to real chained output is the per-TD buffer
window description inside the header (concat's declares its banks; a
fabricated 10-word mirror does not describe windows the DMAs use).

Next work item recorded (prefill width batching, no chaining needed): the
hand-built descriptor's Common InDim/OutDim are (1,1); setting width W turns
the matvec into a W-column matmul sharing one weight blob per submit -
dispatch count per prefill token divides by W. That is the mechanism behind
the macOS oMLX prefill crossover receipt (9.92 percent over GPU, 27B) and
is portable to the verified Linux descriptor.

Prefill width-batching probe (2026-08-28, night, /tmp/gemm-batch.py design
in receipts). Naive scaling of Common InDim/OutDim to W=4 on the verified
512x256 descriptor: W=1 stays exact (max_err 0.0003); W=4 computes column 0
only (cols 1-3 stay sentinel) with w-mode, and corrupts column 0 with
h-mode. Per-submit cost rises 0.238 -> 0.38 ms, so the engine accepted the
geometry but the multi-pixel src/dst DMA layout does not follow InDim
alone. Missing layer: the multi-pixel pixel-interleave in SrcFmt/Srcpad0/
DstFmt and the row/plane strides the compiler picks for compiled shapes.
This is the same RE layer as the chaining blocker. Recorded as the next
experiment: dump src/dst layouts from a compiled oMLX ANE procedure (macOS)
or sweep SrcFmt interleave bits on Linux.

Also fixed en route: ane-gemm-fast.py had lost its verify()/bench() bodies
in an earlier scripted rewrite (the committed G1/G2 evidence predates the
loss); both restored and re-proven on the healthy device (identity ok,
max_err 0.0003, PERSISTENT_GEMM_VERIFY_OK).

Format sweep for width batching (2026-08-28, late). 12-config sweep on the
verified 512x256 descriptor at W=4 (src layouts depth/chmaj/pxmaj x
SrcFmt intrlv bit 0x...31 vs 0x...11 x dst read layouts depth/px-packed):
- EVERY config computes exactly column 0; columns 1-3 never leave the
  sentinel. The output DMA writes one 0x8000 pixel window regardless of
  InDim/OutDim width or SrcFmt interleave.
- intrlv=0x...11 breaks even column 0 (intrlv bit is required for the
  verified single-pixel path).
- chmaj src corrupts column 0 (0.816 err), confirming the src bank window
  IS being read from the first 0x4000 only.
Conclusion: Common-section width does not plumb into the DMAs on this
hand-built descriptor. Multi-pixel batching requires a compiler-generated
descriptor (dump an oMLX ANE procedure on macOS and port its Common/DMA
fields) - recorded as the next work item. The bit sweep is negative
evidence; do not re-sweep these 12 combos.

Compiler ground truth + sweep v3 (same night). Compiled net-local.plist
with InputWidth 4 on macOS 26 (aneccompile, clean compile) and diffed the
TD against the w1 golden. Real compiler w1->w4 changes (conv family):
- DstDMAConfig 0xc1 -> 0x40000c1: BIT 26 = multi-pixel dst enable
- Cfg 0x4144405 -> 0xc4405: bit 26 CLEARED
- engines 0x220/0x224 0x30 -> 0x0
- ConvResultChannelStride 0x10 -> 0x0, ConvResultRowStride 0x30 -> 0x0
- Srcpad1 0x0 -> 0xc0 (= SrcDepthStride)
- InDim/OutDim (1,1) -> (1,4); SrcDepthStride UNCHANGED (Cin*0x40)
- depth strides = channels * 0x40 exactly (src 3*0x40=0xc0, dst Cout*0x40)

Porting the flag to the gemm descriptor (DstDMAConfig bit 26 with
InDim/OutDim w=4, 4 combos of result-stride/engines/srcpad1): ALL TIMEOUT.
The bit changes the dst DMA addressing mode and wedges the conv-family
descriptor unless the full compiler-consistent field set is ported - the
fields interact per family. Conclusion stands: batching needs a compiler-
generated descriptor or a complete family-consistent port; bit-sweeping
individual fields is exhausted (sweep v1: 12 configs, v2: 4, v3: 4 - all
recorded). Device recovered after (reboot + ladder, ANE_VERIFIED).

Header theory eliminated (2026-08-28, night close-out). Diffed the 0x28-byte
task headers: the macOS 26 compiler DOES emit the full task header in the
fresh HWX, structurally identical to the old format (W0t=0x02000000,
exe_cycles 0x42a, W6=0x30009800 all present and plausible). The fresh-TD
execution wall is therefore NOT a missing header - it is exactly the
20-word TD delta measured earlier: zeroed engine-count words (0x220/0x224,
same cluster the compiler clears for multi-pixel), the Cfg bit-26 family,
and stride words. Every open thread (fresh-TD execution, gemm chaining,
width batching) now converges on ONE remaining RE task: the semantics of
that TD field cluster in the macOS 26 output. Once decoded, all three
capabilities unlock together. Localized, recorded, and parked.

CHAIN PROTOCOL DECODED (2026-08-28, night, session 3). Found the compiler's
own TD register map: `lib/aneregs.json` in tinygrad-ane-v0103 (full bitfield
names for the TD header). Decoded concat's chained pair with it:
- W0 bits 0-15 = TID (task/chain index: 0 then 1)
- W0 bit 24 = LNID, bit 25 = EON - set on the LAST TD only
- W3 bits 0-15 = NextSize = 156 (0x9c) on non-last TDs, 0 on the last
- W7 = NextPointer, W6 low bits = NextPriority; W8 = RBase0/RBE0/RBase1/
  WBase/WBE/TBase/TBE/ENE bank enables (RBase0 6->5 and TBE 1->0 differ
  between concat's two TDs - per-TD bank assignment is real)
With the protocol applied (TID 0/1, EON+LNID on the last, NextSize 156/0),
the 2-TD GEMM chain NO LONGER TIMES OUT: the device executes both TDs
end-to-end. Remaining defect: output windows contain all-inf - the chained
task's kernel-bank addressing (per-TD kernel field 0x81000000+k*0x80000 vs
bar[KRN]) does not yet land on the programmed blobs, or the stab must
declare the kernel region for chained tasks. Next probe (cheap, decisive):
single-TD gemm with kernel field offset 0x80000 and blob at cmd 0x580+
0x80000 - tests bank-field offsets beyond zero in isolation.

aneregs.json header map (byte,bit,width): W0 TID[0,0,16] NID[2,0,8]
LNID[3,0,1] EON[3,1,1]; W1 ExeCycles[4,0,16]; W3 NextSize[6,0,9];
W6 NextPriority[26,0,6] TDSkip[25,1,1] DisallowAbort[25,0,1];
W7 NextPointer[28,0,32]; W8 RBase0[32,0,5] RBE0[32,5,1] RBase1[32,6,5]
WBase[33,4,5] WBE[34,1,1] TBase[34,2,5] TBE[34,7,1] ENE[35,0,3];
W9 KBase0[36,0,5] KBE0[36,5,1] KBase1[36,6,5] KBE2[38,1,1] ...
